Had not realized we already have the automatic  behaviour on the `otherwise`, but obv that does not solve the issue you are running into that an unfinished `WhenThen` expression and a normal expression are not the same still. 

@ritchie46 : there are pro's and cons, just pointing out that we have a very special construct here which is the driving the issue when it comes to typing, and that one could think of alternatives that do not have that problem, but come with other weaknesses.

A bit off-topic, but given the requests for it before, I think there is room for a `Expr.whenthen(<if>, <value if true>, <value if False, default=expr>)` in addition to what there is, we should not necessarily have to choose one over the other. I.e., right now there is support for `if {branch} else {branch}` , and the automatic addition of `otherwise(pl.lit(None))` means there is also `if {branch} else {None}` supported, but not for `if {branch}`. Of course, one can do `pl.when(expr1).then(<value True>).otherwise(expr2).alias(expr2.meta.output_name())` but `expr2.whenthen(<value True>, expr1)` is much more readable.
